-> The Difference b/w Spring and Spring Boot:
    - In Spring we do configurations with xml; whereas in Spring Boot, we do configurations using annotations.
    - even Spring too supports annotations. and Spring Boot it completely works only with annotations.

// NOTE ---------
    ( Singleton Design Pattern:
            It says, 1 class -> 1 object (multiple objects should not be created for a single class,
                                          instead use a same object and share the same object to multiple requests)
            - for that we have to mention the constructor in the class as 'private' and we have to declare the object in the class
                and we create a public getter method and return that declared object following few rules like,
                putting a null check, where only if the check is null we create the object otherwise we return the existing object.
    The two rules -
        -> make the constructor 'private'
        -> write a static method which returns the instance.
        -> whenever we need object , we just call that static method.
    )
    // ----------

    -- "Spring entirely works on 'Singleton Design Pattern'" --
           -By default all classes in Spring are Singleton.

- Beans = Objects (in spring)

- Spring handles beans. i.e. objects are not created by us, instead spring does.

-> IOC - Inversion Of Control (as the control of handling bean(object) is inversed, it is called 'Inversion of Control')
    - Spring uses a container to manage this (IoC container). It stores Beans.

    -> 2 Types of containers :
        1. BeanFactory
        2. ApplicationContext (it is advanced version of BeanFactory. and we generally use ApplicationContext)
            - ApplicationContext is an Interface.
            - As it is an interface there are two classes which provides it's implementation.
                -> 1. ClassPathXMLApplicationContext - it is used when we are configuring everything using XML.
                   2. AnnotationConfigApplicationContext - it is used when we are configuring using annotations.

